\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.4}$\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris: an astrophysical positive control, and the obstacle it identifies}{3}{subsection.1.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:bpicval}{{1.4}{3}{$\beta $~Pictoris: an astrophysical positive control, and the obstacle it identifies}{subsection.1.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris positive control, in both directions. \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}The spatial screen. One point per execution block in which the blind search recovers circumstellar carbon monoxide, plotted as the stellar statistic against the maximum over that window's 512{} control positions: the 9{} blocks in which the star outranks every control, and one further block in which the ring wins. \emph  {The control is that the search recovers a real narrow line at a stellar position, in two transitions and in every one of these blocks.} It is not that the star outranks the ring everywhere. In block \texttt  {X7116f1\_X1785}, Band\nobreakspace  {}6, the control ring reaches 14.13{} against 9.68{} at the star, with 6{} of the 512{} control positions above it, so the screen rejects a window holding carbon monoxide that is real, centred on the star and independently mapped. The disc is resolved on the scale of the primary beam in that configuration, which is the geometry the screen is built to reject, so \textbf  {the screen measures compactness and not authenticity} (\S  \ref  {sec:bpicval}). \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}Line confusion, which is what makes the screen's job hard. Each crossing is plotted by its velocity offset from the nearest masked transition \emph  {in its own star's rest frame}, with the adopted $\pm 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ half-width marked. The 10{} $\beta $\nobreakspace  {}Pictoris carbon-monoxide crossings land 0.1--0.5\,km\,s$^{-1}$ from CO, at the resolution of the systemic velocity itself, while the two CS($5{\to }4$) crossings of the same star sit 3765--3870\,km\,s$^{-1}$ from anything catalogued and are not attributed at any width. 39{} further crossings are drawn in grey, with HD\nobreakspace  {}48370 marked; 52{} of the 56{} are plotted, the remainder being crossings for which the release stores no control ring. \emph  {A crossing is identified by where it falls in this plane and not by how strong it is}, which is why the line mask and not the trigger is the productive test.}}{4}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bpiccontrol}{{3}{4}{The $\beta $~Pictoris positive control, in both directions. \emph {(a)}~The spatial screen. One point per execution block in which the blind search recovers circumstellar carbon monoxide, plotted as the stellar statistic against the maximum over that window's \NCtrl {} control positions: the \NStageOneBpicEb {} blocks in which the star outranks every control, and one further block in which the ring wins. \emph {The control is that the search recovers a real narrow line at a stellar position, in two transitions and in every one of these blocks.} It is not that the star outranks the ring everywhere. In block \texttt {\BpCtrlBlock }, Band~\BpCtrlBand , the control ring reaches \BpCtrlRingMax {} against \BpCtrlStar {} at the star, with \BpCtrlNAbove {} of the \BpCtrlNCtrl {} control positions above it, so the screen rejects a window holding carbon monoxide that is real, centred on the star and independently mapped. The disc is resolved on the scale of the primary beam in that configuration, which is the geometry the screen is built to reject, so \textbf {the screen measures compactness and not authenticity} (\S \ref {sec:bpicval}). \emph {(b)}~Line confusion, which is what makes the screen's job hard. Each crossing is plotted by its velocity offset from the nearest masked transition \emph {in its own star's rest frame}, with the adopted $\pm \MaskHalfKms $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ half-width marked. The \BpicPanelNCo {} $\beta $~Pictoris carbon-monoxide crossings land \BpicDvCoLo --\BpicDvCoHi \,km\,s$^{-1}$ from CO, at the resolution of the systemic velocity itself, while the two CS($5{\to }4$) crossings of the same star sit \BpicDvCsLo --\BpicDvCsHi \,km\,s$^{-1}$ from anything catalogued and are not attributed at any width. \BpicPanelNOther {} further crossings are drawn in grey, with HD~48370 marked; \BpicPanelNDrawn {} of the \NCross {} are plotted, the remainder being crossings for which the release stores no control ring. \emph {A crossing is identified by where it falls in this plane and not by how strong it is}, which is why the line mask and not the trigger is the productive test}{figure.3}{}}
